window.docContents[50].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Theorem-files/Computational.Theorems.Main/#file-Computational___Theorems___Main":{"contents":"Paper › Computational/Theorems › Main.leanTier 1 · theorem file\n\nTier 1 · theorem file used by the computational proof 0 headline theorems 103 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Read in full. This file states headline theorems. Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.This file is in the non-default library CliElfLib (its theorems need the ELF built from source). Its theorems are not among the headline theorems that the closure tool lists.\n\nCited in the paper* Theorem 2 (Binary security, simplified), §5.2.3: libsignal_cli_pq_secure_elf_binary_placed* Lemma 4 (Machine code refines the model), §C.3: libsignal_cli_pq_binary_correct_placed\n\n\n\nLemma 4 and Main Theorem: Computational Correctness and Security of the libsignal-cli BinaryTrusted: the statement. Built by lake build CliElfLib. Each theorem below states a proposition from Computational/Specifications/ or LibsignalBinary/Specifications/ on the SHA-256-pinned AArch64 binary cliElfBytes (LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliElf.lean) and is proved by a corresponding *_proof theorem in Computational/Proofs/. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md for the trusted definitions and kernel axioms of each theorem, and Computational/docs/DEVIATIONS.md for the specification deviations referenced below.Lemmas 1–3 (Computational/Theorems/Lemmas.lean), relocatable Lemma 4, and freedom from .OutOfMemory in PQXDH mode imply the relocatable Main Theorem (main_of_lemmas_placed, Computational/Proofs/LibsignalCli/MainOfLemmas.lean); for cliElfBytes, .OutOfMemory freedom is discharged unconditionally under the 1 GiB dynamic-mapping budget (DEV-CLI12, retired).* Result* Theorem* Statement* Proof* Lemma 4* libsignal_cli_pq_binary_correct_placed* CliPqxdhBinaryCorrectPlaced cliElfBytes (LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliBinaryPq.lean)* Computational/Proofs/LibsignalCli/ElfHeadlinePq.lean* Main Theorem* libsignal_cli_pq_secure_elf_binary_placed* CliPqxdhBinaryCorrectAndSecurePlaced cliElfBytes (Computational/Specifications/LibsignalCli/StatementPqElf.lean)* Computational/Proofs/LibsignalCli/ElfHeadlinePq.lean* Session placement witness* libsignal_cli_elf_linked_in* libsignalSessionLib.LinkedIn cliElfBytes (LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliBinaryPq.lean)* Computational/Proofs/LibsignalCli/ElfHeadlinePq.lean* Allocator placement witness* libsignal_cli_elf_mallocng_linked_in* VerifiedLibraries.mallocngLib.LinkedIn cliElfBytes (VerifiedLibraries/Mallocng/Spec/PlacedLib.lean)* Computational/Proofs/LibsignalCli/ElfHeadlinePq.lean* Label gate A* libsignal_cli_labels_from_objects* CliLabelsFromObjects (LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliLabels.lean)* Computational/Proofs/LibsignalCli/ElfHeadlinePq.leanTrusted computing base. Lean's kernel checks code bytes, relocations, addresses, and segment layout directly against cliElfBytes (Placed.CliElfCodeMatchesPlaced / InElf, cliElf_linkedAt_unique). Symbol-name labels rest on the trusted .o extractor (libsignal_cli_labels_from_objects, assumption A-LINK in Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md).\n\n\n\ntheorem libsignal_cli_pq_binary_correct_placedL53–62 ↗#Lemma 4 (binary refinement, relocatable) (CliPqxdhBinaryCorrectPlaced cliElfBytes, LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliBinaryPq.lean). For every multi-block placement mpl linked in cliElfBytes (libsignalSessionLib.LinkedAt cliElfBytes mpl), cliElfBytes parses as an AArch64 ELF satisfying Placed.CliElfCodeMatchesPlaced mpl, and for all hook implementations W and model extras X, the relocatable process Placed.cliRunPlacedSig mpl satisfies CliSpecPqxdh and CliSpecPqxdhNoMatch (DEV-CLI11) at primitives ⟨W.C, W.Q⟩ (SPQR V1) or terminates with .OutOfMemory (CliReachesOOMPlaced, DEV-CLI15).\n\ntheorem libsignal_cli_elf_linked_inL64–67 ↗#Session placement witness. cliElfBytes links libsignalSessionLib at a concrete multi-block placement (libsignalSessionLib.LinkedIn cliElfBytes).\n\ntheorem libsignal_cli_elf_mallocng_linked_inL69–75 ↗#Allocator placement witness. cliElfBytes links the musl mallocng allocator library VerifiedLibraries.mallocngLib at a concrete multi-block placement (VerifiedLibraries.mallocngLib.LinkedIn cliElfBytes), discharging both LinkedAt hypotheses (mpl and mmpl) together with libsignal_cli_elf_linked_in.\n\ntheorem libsignal_cli_labels_from_objectsL77–82 ↗#Label gate A (CliLabelsFromObjects, LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/CliLabels.lean). The symbol names interpreted by the placed theorems (hooked externs, SIGABRT panic externs, and SPQR CPU-probe statics) match the symbol names extracted from the pre-link .o objects.\n\n\n\ntheorem libsignal_cli_pq_secure_elf_binary_placedL90–104 ↗#Main Theorem (binary correctness and security, relocatable) (CliPqxdhBinaryCorrectAndSecurePlaced cliElfBytes, Computational/Specifications/LibsignalCli/StatementPqElf.lean). Across every pair of multi-block placements (mpl, mmpl) linked in cliElfBytes (libsignalSessionLib.LinkedAt cliElfBytes mpl, VerifiedLibraries.mallocngLib.LinkedAt cliElfBytes mmpl), cliElfBytes parses as an AArch64 ELF, satisfies CliPqxdhElfCorrectPlaced mpl mmpl (allowing .OutOfMemory per DEV-CLI15), and for every point type Pt, setup S, and plaintext length ℓ satisfies CliPqxdhElfSecurePlaced mpl mmpl for all hooks W and model extras X meeting the primitive hypotheses (with no .OutOfMemory hypothesis; DEV-CLI12, retired).\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Computational/Theorems/Main.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Theorem-files/Computational.Theorems.Main/#file-Computational___Theorems___Main"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/Computational___Specifications___/Computational.Specifications.Session.ACD19.SM.Theorem8And12Statements/#file-Computational___Specifications___Session___ACD19___SM___Theorem8And12Statements":{"contents":"Paper › Computational/Specifications/Session/ACD19/SM › Theorem8And12Statements.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the computational proof 1 headline theorem depend on it 13 of 78 lines trusted trust banner B2, trusted: definitions used by the statement source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nTheorems 8 and 12: authenticity and privacy of the Fig. 8 SM scheme (statements)Trusted: definitions used by the statement. The theorems of Computational/Theorems/ use healEpoch and SM.specDF, the only definitions in this file. SchemeAdversary, Theorem8 and Theorem12 are in Computational/Proofs/SpecLemmas/Session/ACD19/SM/Theorem8And12Statements.lean, checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md §7.In ACD19 (Alwen, Coretti, Dodis, ePrint 2018/1037, §5.3.2–5.3.3, Theorems 8 and 12), the Fig. 8 Double Ratchet secure-messaging scheme (LibsignalBinary/Specifications/CryptoGames/SM/Construction.lean) achieves authenticity and privacy for Δ_SM = 2 + Δ_CKA from a CKA scheme, a root PRF-PRNG, and an FS-AEAD scheme:* Adv^{auth}_{t*_L,t*}(A) ≤ Adv^{CKA}(R₁ A) + Adv^{PP}(R₂ A) + Σ_{j<q} Adv^{fs-aead}(R₃,j A),* `Adv^{priv}_{t*_L,t*}(A) ≤ 2·Adv^{CKA}(R₁ A) + 2·Adv^{PP}(R₂ A) + Adv^{fs-aead}(R₃ A)* 2·Adv^{fs-aead}(R₃' A)`,for q-query adversaries A, attacked epoch t*, last prior leak t*_L, and CKA healing epoch t_heal = t*_L + 2 (healEpoch). Theorem8 and Theorem12 parameterize over the reductions R₁, R₂, R₃, instantiated and proved in Computational/Proofs/CryptoGames/SM/.Authenticity is a winning probability while CKA and PRF-PRNG advantages are real/ideal distances, so each hop costs 1× advantage, and R₃,j attacks the j-th epoch-t* FS-AEAD session and outputs a coin. Privacy is a bias (2·|Pr − 1/2|), so each distance hop costs 2× advantage (DEV-SM20) and the final FS-AEAD hop costs Adv(R₃) + 2·Adv(R₃'), where R₃' outputs a coin on forgery (DEV-SM25); ACD19 writes factor 1 on both bounds and omits the forgery term in Lemma 15.\n\n\n\nabbrev specDFL53–56 ↗#Six-oracle DeliverForge SM specification extending SM.spec with (Party × C × C) →ₒ Option (RecvResult Msg) for distinct ciphertexts sharing wire bytes.\n\n\n\ndef healEpochL64–72 ↗#CKA challenge (healing) epoch t_heal = t*_L + 2, or 0 when t*_L = −∞.DEV-SM27 (formalization): t*_L = −∞. ACD19 (§5.3.2, footnote 13) omits the case with no leak before t*, where the root RNG starts uncorrupted and no CKA step is needed. Setting healEpoch none = 0 places the CKA challenge at epoch 0, which has no challenge and thus zero advantage for every adversary.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"Computational/Specifications/Session/ACD19/SM/Theorem8And12Statements.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/Computational___Specifications___/Computational.Specifications.Session.ACD19.SM.Theorem8And12Statements/#file-Computational___Specifications___Session___ACD19___SM___Theorem8And12Statements"},"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.RecordCodecs.Proto/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___RecordCodecs___Proto":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/RecordCodecs › Proto.leanTier 2 · definitions\n\nTier 2 · definitions used by the symbolic proof 3 headline theorems depend on it 56 of 268 lines trusted trust banner B3, trusted in part source ↗Read the definitions shown. Only the declarations that the headline statements use are shown, with the namespace, section, open and variable commands needed to read them. The collapsed runs are theorems, which Lean checks, and definitions that no headline statement uses.\n\nDeclarations on this page (10)def le64Bytesdef encodeProto3BytesFielddef encodeProto3Uint32Fielddef encodeProto3Fixed64Fieldstructure PreKeyRecordStructuredef encodestructure SignedPreKeyRecordStructuredef encodestructure IdentityKeyPairStructuredef encode\n\nProtobuf Record Codecs for Identity and Pre-Key StoresTrusted in part. SignalSession/PqDir.lean (encodeDir) encodes identity and pre-key store files with IdentityKeyPairStructure, SignedPreKeyRecordStructure, and PreKeyRecordStructure (encode) via encodeProto3BytesField, encodeProto3Uint32Field, encodeProto3Fixed64Field, and le64Bytes; the decoders (empty, mergeField, decode, decodeFieldsLoop, skipUnknown, mergeFixed64, le64) and record wrappers (PreKeyRecord, SignedPreKeyRecord, KyberPreKeyRecord, deserialize, serialize) are checked by Lean. See Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md.Models prost 0.14.4's proto3 encoders (tag-ordered fields, omitting default 0 / empty values) and decoders (Message::merge from default with last-write-wins field merging and unknown-field skipping up to recursion limit 100) for storage.proto messages.\n\n\n\n3 declarations not shown (lines 38–64): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.decodeFieldsLoop, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.skipUnknown, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.le64\n\ndef le64BytesL66–69 ↗#BufMut::put_u64_le: the 8 little-endian bytes of v.\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 71–78): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.mergeFixed64\n\nproto3 field encoders (omit the default value, D/field/scalar.rs:119–126)\n\ndef encodeProto3BytesFieldL82–85 ↗#A proto3 bytes field: nothing when empty, else bytes::encode(tag, value) (P/encoding.rs:691–695).\n\ndef encodeProto3Uint32FieldL87–89 ↗#A proto3 uint32 field: nothing when 0, else uint32::encode(tag, value).\n\ndef encodeProto3Fixed64FieldL91–93 ↗#A proto3 fixed64 field: nothing when 0, else the key and put_u64_le(value).\n\nPreKeyRecordStructure (storage.proto:76–80)\n\nstructure PreKeyRecordStructureL97–106 ↗#message PreKeyRecordStructure { uint32 id = 1; bytes public_key = 2; bytes private_key = 3; } (prost struct proto::storage::PreKeyRecordStructure).\n\n\n\n3 declarations not shown (lines 110–124): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecordStructure.empty, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecordStructure.mergeField, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecordStructure.decode\n\ndef encodeL126–128 ↗#<PreKeyRecordStructure as Message>::encode_to_vec.\n\n\n\nSignedPreKeyRecordStructure (storage.proto:82–88)\n\nstructure SignedPreKeyRecordStructureL134–148 ↗#message SignedPreKeyRecordStructure { uint32 id = 1; bytes public_key = 2; bytes private_key = 3; bytes signature = 4; fixed64 timestamp = 5; }. Both SignedPreKeyRecord (X25519 keys) and KyberPreKeyRecord (KEM keys) store one.\n\n\n\n3 declarations not shown (lines 152–168): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecordStructure.empty, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecordStructure.mergeField, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecordStructure.decode\n\ndef encodeL170–173 ↗#<SignedPreKeyRecordStructure as Message>::encode_to_vec.\n\n\n\nIdentityKeyPairStructure (storage.proto:90–93)\n\nstructure IdentityKeyPairStructureL179–185 ↗#message IdentityKeyPairStructure { bytes public_key = 1; bytes private_key = 2; }.\n\n\n\n3 declarations not shown (lines 189–202): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.IdentityKeyPairStructure.empty, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.IdentityKeyPairStructure.mergeField, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.IdentityKeyPairStructure.decode\n\ndef encodeL204–206 ↗#<IdentityKeyPairStructure as Message>::encode_to_vec.\n\n\n\n9 declarations not shown (lines 212–269): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesCryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecord.preKey, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecord, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecord.deserialize, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.PreKeyRecord.serialize, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecord, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecord.signedPreKey, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecord.deserialize, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.SignedPreKeyRecord.serialize, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.KyberPreKeyRecord.signedPreKey, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.KyberPreKeyRecord, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.KyberPreKeyRecord.deserialize, CryptoVerification.RecordCodecs.KyberPreKeyRecord.serialize\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/RecordCodecs/Proto.lean","id":"/Tier-2___-definitions/LibsignalBinary___Specifications___/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.RecordCodecs.Proto/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___RecordCodecs___Proto"}});